WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.670
Understanding the Linux filesystem hierarchy gives analysts a map for where evidence may live.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:12.371
Linux distributions vary, but many follow common conventions.

00:00:12.371 --> 00:00:30.432
Configuration is often under /etc, logs and variable runtime data under /var, user data under /home, temporary files under /tmp, installed applications under /usr or /opt, and administrative or system binaries under /bin and /sbin.

00:00:30.432 --> 00:00:36.618
Knowing the layout helps analysts search with purpose instead of wandering.

00:00:36.618 --> 00:00:50.225
/etc is especially important because it can reveal system configuration, user and group definitions, service settings, scheduled task definitions, network configuration, package sources, and security-related settings.

00:00:50.225 --> 00:01:03.338
A change in configuration may explain why a service behaved differently, why a user gained access, or why a control did not operate as expected.

00:01:03.338 --> 00:01:08.534
Configuration evidence should be interpreted alongside timestamps and logs.

00:01:08.534 --> 00:01:16.204
/var often contains logs, application data, spool files, caches, package records, and service-specific state.

00:01:16.204 --> 00:01:24.369
Web servers, databases, mail services, package managers, and authentication systems may all leave evidence there.

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:32.534
/home can contain user documents, shell histories, SSH material, local configuration, downloads, and application-specific artifacts.

00:01:32.534 --> 00:01:40.203
/tmp may contain temporary files that are short-lived but sometimes useful when collected quickly.

00:01:40.203 --> 00:01:42.430
Distribution differences matter.

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:54.059
Debian, Ubuntu, Red Hat, Rocky, SUSE, Alpine, cloud images, containers, and appliances may organize logs, packages, network settings, and service configuration differently.

00:01:54.059 --> 00:02:03.708
The analyst should identify the distribution, version, system role, and logging configuration before assuming where evidence must be.

00:02:03.708 --> 00:02:09.893
The hierarchy is a guide, not a substitute for system-specific understanding.
